                and additionalEvent is absent
        else if additionalEvent exists
        then reset is absent
                and change is absent
                and optionExercise is absent
                and optionExpiry is absent
                and optionEvent is absent
                and originatingEvent is absent
                and terminatingEvent is absent
        else False

type LimitApplicable: <"Specifies the details of a credit limit applied to a financial transaction.">

    limitType LimitType (1..1) <"Standard code to indicate which type of credit line is being referred to - i.e. IM, DV01, PV01, CS01, Notional, Clip Size, Notional, maximumOrderQuantity">
    clipSize int (1..1) <"Represents the maximum allowable amount or quantity for a specific limit type. The clipSize ensures that any transaction or position does not exceed the specified limit, thereby aiding in risk management and compliance with internal or regulatory requirements.">
    amountUtilized int (0..1) <"Represents the amount of the credit limit that has been used. This element helps in monitoring and managing exposure by indicating how much of the available credit has been utilized.">
    utilization CreditLimitUtilization (0..1) <"Credit limit utilization breakdown by executed trades and pending orders.">
    amountRemaining int (0..1) <"Represents the remaining amount of a credit limit that has not been utilized.">
    currency Currency (0..1) <"Specifies the currency in which the credit limit is denominated.">
    velocity Velocity (0..1) <"Specifies the frequency at which a credit limit is utilized or replenished.">

    condition Choice:
        optional choice amountUtilized, utilization

type LimitId: <"Uniquely identifies a credit limit within a financial institution\'s system. Its structure allows for the consistent and standardized representation of credit limit identifiers across different systems and platforms.">

    value NonEmptyScheme (1..1)
    creditLimitIdScheme NonEmptyURI (0..1)

type LimitType: <"Specifies the type of credit limit being referenced. It includes an attribute named creditLimitTypeScheme, which is a URI that points to the coding scheme used to define the credit limit type. This structure allows for the standardized representation of various credit limit types, such as IM (Initial Margin), DV01 (dollar value of 1 basis point), PV01 (price value of 1 basis point), CS01 (credit spread of 1 basis point), Notional, Clip Size, and maximum order quantity. By utilizing the LimitType complex type, financial institutions can effectively categorize and manage different types of credit limits within their systems.">

    value Scheme (1..1)
    creditLimitTypeScheme NonEmptyURI (0..1)

type OptionExpiryBase: <"A structure describing an option expiring.">

    tradeIdentifier PartyTradeIdentifier (1..*) <"Uniquely identifies the trade associated with the expiring option.">
    date zonedDateTime (1..1) <"Specifies the calendar date on which the option expires.">
    time time (1..1) <"Records the exact time of expiration, typically in a specified time zone, ensuring precision in tracking the expiration event.">

type PackageHeader: <"Identifying information for a tradePackage (a bundle of trades).">

    packageIdentifier IssuerTradeId (0..1) <"Uniquely identifies a trade package, which is a bundle of trades executed together. It consists of an issuer, representing the entity assigning the identifier, and a tradeId, which is the unique reference for the package within that issuer\'s system.">
    orderIdentifier OrderIdentifier (0..1) <"Represents a unique reference to an order associated with a trade package. It consists of an orderId, which uniquely identifies the order within a trading system or marketplace.">
    packageType PackageType (0..*) <"Classification of the package. FpML has defined a list of coding scheme values. Different organizations might have different naming schemes for the packages so the multiplicity of unbounded is meant to support synonyms but it does not mean that a trade has multiple package types.">
    size PositiveDecimal (0..1) <"The total quantity or notional amount of the trade package, expressed as a positive decimal value.">
    originatingEvent OriginatingEvent (0..1) <"This may be used to describe why a package was created. This can be used to provide context for a newly created package that is not part of a post-trade event. For example, it can report that the package was created as a result of netting activity, or due to a transfer, an allocation process, etc.">
    packageInformation PackageInformation (0..1) <"Provides additional details about the trade package, including related parties, trade categories, execution timing, processing timestamps, allocation and clearing intentions, and execution venue type.">

type TradeMaturity: <"A structure describing a trade maturing.">

    tradeIdentifier PartyTradeIdentifier (1..*) <"Uniquely identifies the trade that has reached its maturity date.">
    date zonedDateTime (1..1) <"Specifies the maturity date of the trade. It represents the date on which the trade reaches its full term or expiration, marking the point when the trade\'s contractual obligations are fulfilled.">

type TradePackage: <"A bundle of trades collected together into a single unit for reporting.">

    packageHeader PackageHeader (0..1) <"Provides essential metadata about a trade package. It includes identifiers, size, event information, and related details that describe the contents of the package. This structure helps in categorizing and organizing the trades into specific packages and provides information necessary for processing or referencing the package.">
    trade Trade (0..*) <"One or more trades, with each trade representing a specific transaction or agreement. It provides the details of the trade, which can be important for analysis or settlement. The use of maxOccurs=\"unbounded\" indicates that a package can contain any number of trades, offering flexibility in how trade data is organized.">
    tradePackageSequence TradePackageSequence (0..*)
    tradeIdentifier TradeIdentifier (0..*) <"Uniquely identifies one or more trades within the package. It ensures that each trade can be traced and associated with its corresponding transaction or agreement.">
    approvals Approvals (0..1) <"A container for approval states in the workflow.">

    condition Choice:
        required choice trade, tradePackageSequence, tradeIdentifier

type TradePackageSequence:

    tradeReferenceInformation TradeReferenceInformation (1..1) <"Information about a trade.">

type TradeReferenceInformation: <"Defines a type that allows trade identifiers and/or trade information to be represented for a trade.">

    originatingEvent OriginatingEvent (0..1) <"This may be used to describe why a trade was created. This can be used to provide context for a newly created trade that is not part of a post-trade event. For example, it can report that the trade was created as a result of netting activity, or due to a transfer, an allocation process, etc. Omitting this implies that the trade record was created as a result of a negotiated new trade.">
    terminatingEvent TerminatingEvent (0..1) <"This may be used to describe why a trade was terminated.">
    partyTradeIdentifier PartyTradeIdentifier (1..*) <"This allows the acknowledging party to supply additional trade identifiers for a trade underlying a request relating to a business event.">
    partyTradeInformation PartyTradeInformation (0..*) <"This allows the acknowledging party to supply additional trade information about a trade underlying a request relating to a business event.">
    productType ProductType (0..1) <"Specifies the classification of the financial product associated with a trade. It provides a standardized way to categorize trades based on predefined taxonomies.">
    productId ProductId (0..1) <"Uniquely identifies the financial product associated with a trade. It provides a standardized identifier that can be used across systems for tracking and reference.">

    condition Choice:
        optional choice originatingEvent, terminatingEvent

type Velocity: <"The speed at which an underlying reference, such as a price or rate, changes over a defined time period. It is commonly used in financial contracts and derivative calculations where rate-of-change metrics are relevant.">

    periodMultiplier int (1..1) <"Specifies the number of time units used to measure velocity. It is an integer value that, when combined with the period element, defines the total duration over which velocity is calculated.">
    period PeriodTimeEnum (1..1) <"Defines the time unit over which velocity is calculated. It is an enumerated value that can represent standard financial time periods (\"D\" for days, \"W\" for weeks, \"M\" for months, \"Y\" for years) or finer time intervals (\"Hour,\" \"Minute,\" \"Second\") for high-frequency measurements.">
